  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nAn upper low continues to progress across Minnesota and will \nslowly progress into the Great Lakes region later this week. \nThis will keep North Dakota in upper level northwesterly flow \nthe next few days. Short range models have low stratus moving \nout of the region by this afternoon. Cyclonic flow aloft could \nlead to some showers in the James River Valley today. Surface \nhigh pressure is forecast to move into the region later today \nwhich will help push clouds eastward. The surface low over \nsouthern Minnesota and surface high over eastern Montana will \nlead to breezy conditions across the eastern half of the state. \nWinds will die down as surface high pressure moves over North \nDakota tonight. A cooler airmass will settle into the region \nbringing forth slightly cooler temperatures. High temperatures \nare forecast to be in the 40s while low temperatures are \nforecast to be in the mid 20s out west to the lower 30s across \nthe east. \n\nUpper level ridging will build back over the Northern Plains by\nthe middle of this week as troughing moves east. This will \nbring forth dry and warm conditions through the remainder of the\nweek. High temperatures this week are forecast to sit in the \n50s across the state with light winds. Ridging will move \neastward this weekend as an upper level low digs across the \nCanadian Prairies. The aforementioned upper level low will bring\nforth windy conditions across the western half of the state\nSaturday. No precipitation is forecast from this upper low. \nRidging will likely build back over western CONUS to start of\nnext week, however northwesterly flow will bring multiple \nsystem through Canada, likely too far north for precipitation.",
